the coffee harvesting season has begun  in South Sudan until now these farmers  near the border with the Democratic  Republic of Congo produced coffee for  the local market many welcome the  opportunity to sell it abroad it's much  better to harvest the coffee for export  here we just take it to the mill and get  paid that's it  exporting the coffee is easier than  drawing it on your own and then having  to look for a buyer here in South Sudan  the world's largest food corporation  Nestle has invested more than half a  million euros here that money has paid  for setting up three cooperatives  training farmers and teachers and  cultivating coffee plants for the first  plantations in the country the coffee is  still dried on site then the raw product  is sent to Switzerland where it's  roasted and ground and packed into  capsules the farmers themselves say they  can't afford this kind of coffee time to  harvest the coffee but then I also have  to go and take care of my subsistence  farm to grow food for my family food  prices are going up I can still just  about afford to buy some oil and salt at  the market over 90% of South Sudan's  land is said to be fertile but most of  the food consumed is imported at high  cost or provided by aid agencies that  makes consumers especially sensitive to  price fluctuations on the global market  many are dependent on charitable aid the  yayyyy agricultural training centre  wants to change that the local NGO is  financed by Norway trainers show farmers  how to make their fields more profitable  for the yeas principle coffee is simply  a byproduct  priority of the private sector is first  of all to fulfill the needs of the local  demand then that means that the next  step is now to go for cash crops that  can be imported to the outside countries  or international Nestle plans to invest  a further two million euros in South  Sudan the company hopes to train more  farmers to grow coffee for export
 

